<p>Luke Fenton-Glynn, a philosopher at Monash University, and before that, University College London, has died.<span id="more-57799"></span></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class=" wp-image-57800 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fenton-glynn-luke-photo.png" alt="" width="725" height="500" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fenton-glynn-luke-photo.png 980w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fenton-glynn-luke-photo-300x207.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fenton-glynn-luke-photo-768x530.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/fenton-glynn-luke-photo-400x276.png 400w" sizes="(max-width: 725px) 100vw, 725px" /></p>
<p>Dr. Fenton-Glynn worked in philosophy of science, metaphysics, and epistemology, with a focus on causation, probability, and peer disagreement. He is the author of <em>Causation </em>(Cambridge, 2021) and various articles, which you can learn more about <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/luke-fenton-glynn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>He moved to Monash University in 2023 after ten years at University College London. Prior to that, he held postdoctoral positions at the Munich Center for Mathematical Philosophy, California Institute of Technology, and the University of Konstanz. He earned his D.Phil. and B.Phil. in philosophy, and a B.A. in philosophy, politics, and economics, at the University of Oxford.</p>
<p>Luke Fenton-Glynn died in June of cancer. He was 43.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/07/08/luke-fenton-glynn-1982-2026/">Luke Fenton-Glynn (1982-2026)</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>